# Third-party Python packages the CANONICAL TEST HARNESS needs.
#
# THIS FILE IS THE DECLARATION. `requirements-harness.txt` beside it is the generated, fully pinned and
# hashed lock. Neither a package name nor a version may appear in `.github/workflows/`: the workflow
# installs from the lock and nothing else, which is asserted by
# `scripts/test-python-dependency-closure.js`.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. The first hosted CI run (32752370139) failed on `ModuleNotFoundError` for
# `requests` and `pandas`. The suite had been green on the developer's workstation for one reason only:
# that machine carried those packages in its global interpreter. Nothing in the repository declared them.
#
# HOW THE CLOSURE IS MEASURED, since the first attempt got it wrong three ways. The checker follows
# local-module imports TRANSITIVELY, reads FUNCTION-LEVEL imports through a full AST walk, and includes
# Python scripts that plan commands SPAWN -- discovered dynamically from command source, never from a
# hard-coded list.
#
# Regenerate the lock with:
#
#   pip-compile --generate-hashes --strip-extras \
#       --output-file=requirements-harness.txt requirements-harness.in
#
# Lock provenance, recorded so a regeneration is comparable: generated with CPython 3.14.3,
# pip-tools 7.6.1, pip 25.3, on win32. The workflow pins the same interpreter version.
#
# scripts/verify_python_environment.py parses requirement markers with pip's VENDORED packaging
# (pip._vendor.packaging), because adding a `packaging` distribution to the lock in order to check
# the lock would be circular. That is a coupling to a private module path, so the verifier refuses
# to run unless the interpreter's pip is exactly the version on the next line. Moving this line is
# how that coupling gets re-reviewed on purpose instead of discovered by a broken run.
# harness-env: pip==25.3

# scripts/test-analytics-credential-containment.ts SPAWNS four tools -- smoke-test.py:33,
# canary-reliability-disclosures.py:34, eval/stress-test.py:26, eval/run-eval.py:27 -- each importing
# requests at MODULE level, above the --print-target early exit. Those tools are `network-optional`
# exclusions, but a REGISTERED test executes them, so the import must resolve.
requests

# scripts/test_historical_core.py imports historical_core, and its tests import import_historical at
# FUNCTION level (test_historical_core.py:1254); import_historical.py:15-16 imports pandas and numpy.
pandas
numpy

# DELIBERATELY ABSENT: anthropic.
#
# eval/stress-test.py:112 and eval/run-eval.py:288 import it INSIDE functions that the --print-target
# path never reaches; measured, all four tools resolve --print-target with anthropic absent. The
# exclusion is therefore CALL-PATH SPECIFIC, not a blanket judgement about the file: it holds only while
# every harness-reachable invocation of those two tools passes --print-target. The closure checker
# enforces exactly that, so a reachable invocation without the flag invalidates the exclusion and makes
# anthropic required.
